WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.440
When looking at this irreplaceable, unique, 
one of&nbsp;a kind medieval city core,

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.600
dating back to the 9th century AD,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:38.978
city leadership had asked themselves 
a&nbsp;crucial question: where do I put my SUV?

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.440
Turns out, invaluable historical heritage sites&nbsp;
are the perfect places to store your car.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.960
The romantic, millennium-old, winding streets&nbsp;
offer an exciting variety of parking spaces.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.160
The centuries-old, baroque squares, formerly home&nbsp;
to bustling markets and high medieval culture,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.840
are now proud displays of contemporary Prague culture.
The same can be said about the baroque side&nbsp;streets,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:10.480
no less ornate than the squares. Offering&nbsp;
a window into the soul of 21st century Prague.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.920
It has been said that Prague is&nbsp;
an utterly dysfunctional city,

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.240
unable to implement any positive changes;&nbsp;
with its Kafka-esque institutions

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.852
slowly collapsing under their own weight. This&nbsp;
is a false sentiment. Changes are, in fact,&nbsp;on the way.

00:01:24.852 --> 00:01:30.000
For example, in terms of traffic policy, the&nbsp;
meeting to determine the date of the session,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.080
on which they decide what cookies to serve on the&nbsp;
first twenty rounds of consultations before the

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.400
preliminary session preceding the initial rounds&nbsp;
of talks, is already scheduled for the year 2046.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.080
In the meantime, Prague's castle remains a symbol of&nbsp;
the values and competence of its leadership.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.560
But of course, Prague is more than&nbsp;
just a castle utilized as a parking lot.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.160
On the other side of the Vltava river we see&nbsp;
Old Town. Behind it is New Town, named so

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.166
due to it having been built in the 14th century.&nbsp;
The two sides are linked by the famous Charles&nbsp;Bridge.

00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:06.080
A masterpiece of baroque engineering and&nbsp;
architecture, built in 1402. Now, Prague’s

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:12.474
leadership had asked themselves: how could we best&nbsp;
utilize a medieval, six-centuries-old bridge in the 21st century?

00:02:12.474 --> 00:02:20.456
Well, it turns out, six-centuries-old, UNESCO-protected&nbsp;
medieval bridges make for great covered parking lots.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.600
We’re almost in Old Town. We just need to cross under&nbsp;
this exquisite baroque bridge tower overlooking

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:34.951
the baroque facades of Old Town, 
right in front of the baroque pedestrian crossing.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.880
The road we’re crossing right now is the famous&nbsp;
Smetana embankment. The presence of cars on this

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.120
road is a recurring point of conflict&nbsp;
between people and the city leadership.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.280
Proponents of traffic calming had argued&nbsp;
that it's likely not a good idea to let a

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.520
dozen people in cars hold up hundreds&nbsp;
of pedestrians and tram passengers at a time.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.960
The city leadership used to respond&nbsp;
to that argument that this state of&nbsp;affairs

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.080
is necessary, as those dozen drivers do not&nbsp;
have a convenient alternative route.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.600
In order to solve this issue, a massive highway&nbsp;
tunnel complex was built under the castle, costing

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.360
2.7 billion dollars in today’s money. At long&nbsp;
last providing a quick, convenient alternative

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.480
to the embankment. Thus proponents of traffic&nbsp;
calming were able to once again raise the issue

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.440
of restricting car traffic on the embankment&nbsp;
in favor of public transit and pedestrians.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.720
City Hall was quick to issue a&nbsp;
response to this renewed request,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.760
later amending it with an additional&nbsp;
statement. It seems that limiting

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:41.360
car traffic on Prague’s Smetana embankment&nbsp;
will remain a task for future generations.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.320
We penetrate into Old Town. Marveling at the&nbsp;
astounding architecture surrounding us. The

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.080
beautiful facades. Saint Clement’s Cathedral.&nbsp;
The Thai Massage Salon next to Saint Clement’s&nbsp;Cathedral.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.000
With a tasteful animatronic clown demon&nbsp;
above it, waving a flag that reads "I LOVE RELAX,"

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.240
with outdoor Christmas lights attached for better visibility.
However,&nbsp;as we were visiting during the off-season,

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.280
unfortunately we were unable to get the&nbsp;
full cultural experience this street offers.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.320
This street is usually a great place to immerse&nbsp;
yourself in Prague culture. We are just

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.798
at the foot of Saint Clement’s Cathedral,&nbsp;
and behind me in the corner there is

00:04:17.798 --> 00:04:19.453
a promotional riksha,

00:04:19.453 --> 00:04:24.000
advertising the Thai massage salon by St Clement’s&nbsp;
Cathedral.

00:04:26.834 --> 00:04:32.560
In this pedestrian zone there is&nbsp;usually a Dodge Ram
American pickup truck&nbsp;parked illegally

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.920
on most days of the year. Though it&nbsp;
doesn't seem to be here on this very day.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.720
Walking down these historical streets, we are&nbsp;
enamored by the vibrant, authentic culture of

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:49.680
Old Town. Full of people: all of them locals&nbsp;
- in their respective home countries. Stores

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:55.840
line the streets, offering basic necessities for&nbsp;
daily life, like absinthe and foreign currency.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.680
But this cultural epicenter of the Czech&nbsp;
Republic is not without its problems.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.080
Short-term rentals on an industrial scale are&nbsp;
increasingly taking over the local housing market.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.440
But what are the locals’ views&nbsp;
on the spread of AirBnB, and what

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.600
do they think of the rumors of eastern&nbsp;
oligarchs buying up the local housing stock,

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.520
to operate entire residential&nbsp;
blocks as short-term rentals?

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.640
These are the questions we wanted&nbsp;
to pose to Old Town's homeowners,

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:22.720
though unfortunately nobody on our&nbsp;
staff spoke Russian or Chinese.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.825
We gradually leave the charm of Old&nbsp;
Town behind us as we enter New Town.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.600
We eventually surface at Wenceslas Square:&nbsp;
the main public space of the Czech Republic.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.720
The square is currently under reconstruction.&nbsp;
Before the works, the square functioned as the

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:47.680
nation’s main, ceremonial parking lot. Over&nbsp;
there we see the statue of Saint Wenceslaus,

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.880
scanning the square with a stern look. Meant to&nbsp;
symbolize the difficult, often futile search for a

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:59.520
free parking spot in Prague, when trying&nbsp;
to park your third car in the city center.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.080
The reconstruction of Wenceslas Square will bring&nbsp;
back the tramline that used to extend all the way

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.440
to the bottom of the square, eliminated by Soviet&nbsp;
leadership in 1980. Following the reconstruction,

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:12.880
trams will return to the upper half of&nbsp;
the square; with plans to restore tracks

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.360
on the lower half having been successfully&nbsp;
blocked by Prague’s conservative leadership.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.920
Beyond the statue of Saint Wenceslas stands&nbsp;
the Czech National Museum. In order to help

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.720
highlight this important building, a 6-to-8&nbsp;
lane urban freeway was built around it.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.400
This is the North-South Prague urban freeway,&nbsp;
built by Soviets to make invasions easier,

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.920
in case locals start wanting&nbsp;things 
like freedom of speech or democracy. Now,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.184
there have been multiple attempts to calm traffic 
on this freeway, by reducing the number of lanes,

00:06:40.184 --> 00:06:44.160
opponents of traffic calming however argue,&nbsp;
that reducing the number of lanes on this highway

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.480
built by the Czechoslovakian Communist&nbsp;
Party would be an instance of Communism.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.080
A stateless, classless road with no currency.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:59.120
This road had been the site to numerous protests&nbsp;
by Last Generation Prague, advocating for a city-wide 30km/h

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:04.240
speed limit. Their actions would see&nbsp;
one of the six-to-eight lanes closed from traffic during

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:10.400
rush hour, leading to a massive traffic jam,&nbsp;
affecting thousands of average, working people.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.240
Although their protests are a rare&nbsp;occurrence, 
we seemed to be in luck.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.640
And yes, ladies and gentlemen, we seem to be

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:21.520
in luck, there does seem to be a protest&nbsp;
by Last Generation Prague just over there,

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:27.360
we were this lucky to have caught it, just at&nbsp;
this time. So we are going to go check it out.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.160
We rush to the scene, hoping to&nbsp;
catch these selfish, narcissistic

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.360
scum red-handed. These vile eco-fascists,&nbsp;
selfishly holding up thousands of regular,

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.320
working people in traffic jams during rush hour.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.666
So we've been looking for the Last Generation&nbsp;
protesters right here, but we can't seem to find&nbsp;them,

00:07:44.666 --> 00:07:49.040
even though the highway is completely&nbsp;
congested. So we believe that these green

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:53.920
communists have changed tactics in the meantime,&nbsp;
opting to show up for their protest in their cars

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.120
instead of on foot. Truly a vile display of&nbsp;
selfishness from these radical activists.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.080
Recovering from the experience, we decide to&nbsp;
calm our nerves with culture. Lucky for us,

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.880
just on the other side of the urban freeway&nbsp;
stands the Czech National Museum's new&nbsp;building.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:14.610
Would you like to visit the Czech&nbsp;
National Museum just over there? This way!

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.440
This passageway leads not just to the Czech&nbsp;
National Museum, but also to the Czech National

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:32.800
Opera. Julius Caesar in Egypt, I hear, is the&nbsp;
latest opera piece that can be viewed there.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.920
And there we can already see the yellow columns,&nbsp;
those are the Czech National Opera. Behind it is

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:43.600
the Czech National Museum's new building,&nbsp;
so just through this underpass. Careful,

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:49.360
there are puddles of feces all around. That column&nbsp;
is used as a public toilet as we can see. And it

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:57.040
smells like that too. Just so we can reconnect to our&nbsp;
core, human, primal nature before witnessing the

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.097
most important cultural relics of the Czech&nbsp;
Republic. And/or an opera piece, just over&nbsp;there.

00:09:02.097 --> 00:09:08.880
Here we can see the opera visitors have&nbsp;
left a can of Pilsner Urquell on the sidewalk.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.040
They must have been in a hurry. Always be on&nbsp;
time when going to an opera, as it's considered

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.897
to be very bad manners to be late. And you might&nbsp;
even have to throw away your beer can on the way.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.240
Once you're done enjoying the greatest cultural&nbsp;
relics and artifacts of the Czech Republic,

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:44.160
you can continue your cultural experience&nbsp;
here, on the outside. Looking at this

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:51.440
SUV parked in the forbidden zone. Over there&nbsp;
there is a Smart car advertising a nightclub [strip club],

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:56.240
which has been left here for quite some time as&nbsp;
we can see. There are stickers on the window,

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:00.591
on the other side there are tags, and&nbsp;
there is dust collecting all over the&nbsp;place.

00:10:00.591 --> 00:10:05.040
And just over there, there is the&nbsp;
rent-a-Hummer, a promotional Hummer which

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:11.120
has been left there for the past two years,&nbsp;
to advertise a rental service locally.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:15.360
Wwe head out towards the center of New&nbsp;
Town in search of greener pastures.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:47.520
We are in Prague’s 2nd district, on&nbsp;
I.P. Pavlova square, where the Prague

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.360
2nd District city hall had come up with&nbsp;
an ingenious urban planning solution,

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.240
to be studied by urban planners across the globe.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:59.240
The car-centric pedestrian zone.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.640
But this isn’t the only instance of&nbsp;
Prague’s urban planning brilliance by far.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.240
We’re standing on Horní street,&nbsp;
in Prague’s 4th district. Where,

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.600
in 2022, the local [district] city hall had turned most&nbsp;
of this sidewalk into a parking lot,

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:24.560
thereby gaining 15 additional parking spaces,&nbsp;
arguing that locals wanted more parking spaces.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:28.640
The locals had filed a lawsuit against the&nbsp;
Fourth District city hall, looking to take

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:33.920
back their sidewalk from cars. Their lawsuit&nbsp;
was successful, with the court ordering city

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:38.320
hall to eliminate the parking spaces.&nbsp;
But this was not the end of the story.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:43.360
City hall has held firm in opposition to the&nbsp;
locals' opposition to adding more parking spaces,

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:46.800
arguing that the locals needed more&nbsp;
parking spaces. They consequently

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:51.520
tried to reintroduce sidewalk parking&nbsp;
in defiance of the court's decision.

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:56.080
Locals sued city hall again, with the court&nbsp;
ordering city hall for the second time to

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.280
remove parking spaces from the sidewalk. It&nbsp;
remains to be seen what city hall will do next.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.240
Will Prague 4's city hall be&nbsp;
able to enact the people's will,

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:08.400
even against the people's&nbsp;
will? Only time will tell.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:11.600
But other Prague districts have been&nbsp;
far more creative in their quest

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:16.240
for the creation of more urban&nbsp;
parking lots in the year 2025.

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:21.120
We are now in the 15th district of Prague,&nbsp;
on Sáňkařská street. Over here we can see

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.040
a number of ingenious solutions enacted by&nbsp;
the local city hall to increase the number

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:29.920
of parking spaces. So as a first step, the&nbsp;
sidewalks on both sides were eliminated,

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:35.200
and turned into parking spaces. And just&nbsp;
to the left of me, which is your right,

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:40.000
there is a children's playground and some trees.&nbsp;
The children's playground will be eliminated,

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:44.720
and these trees will be cut down in&nbsp;
order to create even more parking spaces,

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:49.120
to incentivize an even greater use of automobiles.&nbsp;
By the elimination of the children's playground

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:54.880
and the cutting down of the trees, the&nbsp;
district will gain 23 new parking spaces.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:58.800
Locals have created a petition against&nbsp;
the measure, already passed by city hall.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.360
It remains to be seen if Prague 15 will be&nbsp;
more successful than Prague 4 in enacting the

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:08.160
people's will against the people's will. But&nbsp;
turning sidewalks and children's playgrounds

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:12.400
into parking lots are not the only measures&nbsp;
Prague has in store for us. A further pillar

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:16.560
of Prague's urban planning policy is the&nbsp;
complete lack of traffic rule enforcement.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:19.840
The threat of actually enforcing the&nbsp;
rules of the road has naturally made a

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.520
lot of middle-aged men in large,&nbsp;
expensive cars extremely upset.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:27.200
They worry that if the rules of the road&nbsp;
suddenly applied to them as well, then lively

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:32.800
streets such as this one would turn into urban&nbsp;
hellscapes, like the ones in Amsterdam or Vienna.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:37.520
However, some locals continue to entertain&nbsp;
notions, such as: drivers should abide by

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:42.720
the rules of the road; further polarizing society&nbsp;
by putting themselves in opposition to those who

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:47.520
want to ignore [the rules]. In order to resolve this&nbsp;
conflict, a compromise was struck by Prague's

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:52.720
city hall. On one hand, drivers can break any&nbsp;
traffic rule with impunity. On the other hand,

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:58.720
pedestrians, cyclists and public transit&nbsp;
users may shut up. Still, many of Prague's

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:03.520
drivers continue to complain that they are, in&nbsp;
fact, being oppressed. Drivers in Prague are

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:08.160
indeed so oppressed, that illegal drag races&nbsp;
in the city center can only be held at night,

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:13.040
whereby contestants block the main urban&nbsp;
freeway leading into the center. The police

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:17.680
haven't yet mustered the courage to intervene,&nbsp;
preferring to watch from a safe distance.

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:21.920
Parking, traffic calming and pedestrian safety&nbsp;
aren't the only hot button issues in Prague,&nbsp;however.

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:25.120
A further tumultuous point&nbsp;
of contention is the question of

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:29.120
urban cycling. Up until recently, plotting&nbsp;
a safe bicycle route through Prague was

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:33.040
a task impossible for average people.&nbsp;
However, technology has now made this

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:39.174
possible, as long as one can afford&nbsp;
processing time on the IBM mainframe.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:45.200
Prague urban planning works much like&nbsp;
alchemy, in that if you create something good,

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:48.960
another good thing of equal value must be&nbsp;
taken away. So here, in front of Prague's

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:54.400
second largest railway station, the city had&nbsp;
created a pedestrian zone using these bollards,

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:57.520
which is only occasionally violated&nbsp;
by drivers. And so in exchange,

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:02.960
in the next street over there, a bike lane&nbsp;
was taken away to make more space for cars.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:08.640
Yet another defeat for Stalinism in Prague, the&nbsp;
City of Progress. Of course, when Joseph Stalin

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:14.880
first began building safe, separated bike paths in&nbsp;
1924, nobody could have suspected that those are,

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:19.920
in fact, precursors to a totalitarian empire.&nbsp;
Thankfully, Prague's leadership is always on edge,

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:25.200
determined to stop such evils from&nbsp;
ever setting foot within city limits.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:29.360
It's time for us to leave, so we&nbsp;
head to Prague's Central Station,

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:34.080
scheduled to be reconstructed sometime&nbsp;
within the next 5 to 50 years. To that end,

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:37.520
a large part of the currently indoors&nbsp;
passenger hall is to be demolished,

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:44.320
to give way to a massive, open-air wooden&nbsp;
pergola. However, some have questioned the design.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:48.000
Thankfully, the concept of "weather"&nbsp;
as such does not exist in Prague,

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:58.000
with every single day of the year being a very&nbsp;
pleasant 20°C, slightly sunny, and no wind.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:17.440
I think the most frustrating thing about&nbsp;
Prague for me personally, is that everything

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:21.440
is given for a great city, for an absolutely&nbsp;
great city. The buildings are beautiful,

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:25.040
the landmarks are there, it has plenty&nbsp;
of history going back hundreds of years,

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:30.160
and yet, when you look around on the streets,&nbsp;
the city itself does not seem to acknowledge the

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:35.600
fact that only about a quarter of its residents&nbsp;
drive a car on on a day-to-day basis. If you look

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:40.960
at the average Prague streetscape, you would&nbsp;
think that 90% of citizens drive a car. That

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:47.680
is absolutely not the case. And I think Prague&nbsp;
should take more steps to make its public spaces

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:53.600
more fair. But of course the reason why Prague&nbsp;
does not look like Vienna or Amsterdam has its

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:58.560
own political reasons. The city leadership,&nbsp;
where conservatives are in the majority,

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:04.160
have been blocking every single initiative&nbsp;
to make the city more pedestrian-friendly,

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:10.560
cycling-friendly or even to just prioritize public&nbsp;
transit, so that two people in cars cannot hold

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:15.600
up two-to-three hundred in trams or buses. All&nbsp;
of these measures, that would make Prague a more

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:22.800
western city, have been consistently vetoed by the&nbsp;
conservative - I suppose - majority. If Charles IV

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:28.000
came back today, and looked at the current, sorry&nbsp;
state of Prague, Charles IV could not make it to

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:33.920
Prague Castle on the Royal Path. He would get run&nbsp;
over. The current urban planning status quo in

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:40.960
Prague is fundamentally anti-human, anti-culture,&nbsp;
anti-history. Look at the castle. Look at the

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:47.200
state of Old Town, and especially New Town. Cars&nbsp;
parked everywhere, all the streets are congested,

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:53.600
dangerous, kids cannot be let go on bicycles&nbsp;
alone, which is a standard in every single western

00:17:53.600 --> 00:18:00.800
capital, western city. Most of these places are&nbsp;
just three or four bollards away from becoming

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:06.800
really quality, human friendly areas. The city&nbsp;
is under conservative leadership. Conservative

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:12.480
parties seem to hate walkable, cycling-friendly,&nbsp;
pedestrian-friendly urban planning styles. Their

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:18.480
ideal world is one where the inner city is just&nbsp;
an AirBnB desert. It's just a short-term rental

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:23.680
wasteland, full of tourists, no one else, no&nbsp;
locals. And everyone is living outside in the

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:28.960
suburbs, in the satellites, in these tiny, cheap&nbsp;
bungalows, and everyone commutes in by car and

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:34.400
then leaves in the evening. This has a number of&nbsp;
notable negative effects on society. It atomizes

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:41.280
people, it atomizes society. Just individuals and&nbsp;
families, as Margaret Thatcher would say. And I

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:45.040
don't want anyone to take this as me [maliciously]&nbsp;
criticizing Prague or [maliciously] criticizing

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:49.600
the Czech people. That is not my goal at all.&nbsp;
I would like to help improve things, because

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:57.120
obviously, so far nothing has helped in Prague.&nbsp;
I believe in a better Prague, and I believe,

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:03.920
consequently, in a better Czech Republic. And&nbsp;
I think one of the roads towards that is sound

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:09.379
urban planning policy, Which is the exact opposite&nbsp;
of what Prague is doing right now. And my hope is, that

00:19:09.379 --> 00:19:16.960
someone waking up finally in city hall, and&nbsp;
realizing that this cannot go on any further,

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:22.880
things need to change. And that in the end, we&nbsp;
will see a Prague which is truly a western city,

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:31.209
as opposed to something out of a&nbsp;
postcard of 1970s Turkmenistan.

